    <h1 class="rv in" style="font-size:clamp(2.2rem,5vw,4rem);max-width:20ch">Are Boat Trips in Madeira Worth It in Winter?</h1>
    <div class="artmeta rv in d1"><span>14 November 2026</span><span>6 min read</span><span>Funchal · Madeira</span></div>
  </div>
</header>

<section class="pad">
  <div class="wrap">
    <article class="article rv">
      <p class="lede">On the right day, yes. From December to February Funchal&#x27;s afternoons usually reach about 20°C, the low sun lights the cliffs well and sunset trips leave before 17:00 in December, but you lose more days to swell than in summer, and the sea is a fresh 18 to 20°C.</p>

      <h2>What is the weather like on the water from December to February?</h2>
<p>Mild, with more variety than in summer. IPMA's 1991 to 2020 normals for the Funchal observatory give average daily highs of about 20 to 21°C in all three months, and lows of 14 to 15.5°C. That is a long-sleeve day on land and a light-jacket day on a moving boat.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Month</th><th>Average high and low in Funchal</th><th>Sea, roughly</th><th>Days with 1 mm of rain or more</th><th>Sunset, mid-month</th><th>Sunset Trip leaves about</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>December</td><td>21.0°C and 15.5°C</td><td>about 20°C</td><td>8.6</td><td>18:05</td><td>16:45 to 16:50</td></tr>
<tr><td>January</td><td>20.0°C and 14.3°C</td><td>18 to 19°C</td><td>6.2</td><td>18:20</td><td>17:05</td></tr>
<tr><td>February</td><td>19.9°C and 13.9°C</td><td>about 18°C</td><td>5.5</td><td>18:50</td><td>17:35</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>Even in December, fewer than one day in three has measurable rain, and a rainy day in the records can be a single shower. The same normals give Funchal about 151 hours of sunshine in December, 161 in January and 167 in February, so close to five or six hours of sun a day. The bigger change from summer is the state of the sea, not the sky.</p>
<h2>How many winter days are lost to the sea?</h2>
<p>More than in summer, though nobody can give you an honest percentage, because winters differ so much. The island shields Funchal from the trade wind out of the north-east. Winter storms are another matter: they roll in from the west and south-west, straight at the side of Madeira that has no cover. One case: in March 2025, IPMA, the weather service, put out a yellow alert for the south coast because it expected westerly waves of 4 to 5 metres. In December 2025, it went to orange.</p>
<p>Between those systems come calm, clear days with little wind, which are some of the loveliest of the year on the water. The trick is to stay flexible. Book your trip early in your stay, keep a spare day after it, and watch the forecast. When the skipper judges the swell too big, the trip is off, and you pick a new day or take your money back.</p>
<h2>Can you still swim in winter?</h2>
<p>You can, if you like your water fresh. The sea around Funchal is at its warmest in late summer and coolest in February and March, at about 18°C. December still holds around 20°C from the summer. In winter the <a href="/hidden-coves-half-day">Day Trip</a> keeps both of its dips, under the cliffs at Fajã dos Padres and off Ribeira Brava, and the paddle boards still come out.</p>
<p>For most people a winter swim is a short, bracing dip rather than a long float. Bring a towel and a warm top for afterwards, because a breeze on wet skin feels much colder than the water. If you feel the cold easily, be honest with yourself: a winter Day Trip is still worth it for the coast, the food and the drone shots at Cabo Girão, even if you only watch the others jump in. Our <a href="/posts/madeira-sea-temperature-swimming-guide">guide to Madeira's sea temperature</a> has the month-by-month detail.</p>
<h2>Is winter light good for photos?</h2>
<p>Often better than summer light. At Madeira's latitude, the noon sun in late December climbs only about 34 degrees above the horizon. That side light gives the dark basalt of Cabo Girão texture and shadow, and it keeps the colours of Câmara de Lobos warm through the afternoon. Days are short, about ten hours around the winter solstice, so every trip runs in that low, golden angle.</p>
<p>Nothing in the Chifbay package shrinks for winter. You still get the 360° Insta360 video, real camera photos, and drone footage on days calm enough to fly.</p>
<h2>Morning, afternoon or sunset in winter?</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>10:00 Day Trip:</strong> the sun has been up for only about two hours, so the start is cool. Back on the pontoon by 12:30 or 13:00, you still have the whole afternoon.</li>
<li><strong>14:00 Day Trip:</strong> the warmest hours of the day, back at 16:30 or 17:00, before a December sunset at about 18:00. The best choice if you want to swim.</li>
<li><strong>Sunset Trip:</strong> departure tracks the sunset, 1 h 15 ahead of it, which means roughly 16:45 at the start of December and closer to 17:35 by the middle of February. The 2h trip turns at Cabo Girão, the 2h30 at Ribeira Brava, and the ride back is in dusk and then dark. There is no swimming. Dress warmly, as we explain in <a href="/posts/how-cold-is-a-sunset-boat-trip-in-madeira">how cold a sunset trip gets</a>.</li>
</ul>
<h2>What does a winter trip include, and what does it cost?</h2>
<p>The same as in any other month. Your group of up to five has the Chifbay boat and a local skipper to itself, with food on board and drinks that run from local wine and poncha to beer and soft drinks. There is a bathroom on board, which is welcome on a cool day. Prices are per boat, not per person:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Day Trip:</strong> 10:00 or 14:00 start, 500 EUR for 2h30, 600 EUR for 3h.</li>
<li><strong>Sunset Trip:</strong> 400 EUR for 2h, turning at Cabo Girão, or 500 EUR for 2h30, turning at Ribeira Brava.</li>
</ul>
<p>In a season when you will want to read the forecast before you commit, it helps that cancelling costs nothing until 24 hours before the start. Everything about the meeting point at Marina do Funchal is on our <a href="/practical">practical page</a>.</p>
<h2>Who should skip a winter boat trip?</h2>
<p>Three kinds of traveller. If swimming is your main reason to go out and you hate cold water, wait for summer or autumn. If you have only one fixed day on the island and no room to move it, winter weather may let you down, so a calmer season suits you better. And if you get seasick easily, read the swell forecast closely, because winter swell tends to be bigger and longer even on fine days.</p>
<p>For everyone else, winter on the water is quiet, mild and full of light. Winter also has one night summer cannot match: 31 December 2026, when the boat stays out from 22:00 to 01:00 for <a href="/new-years-eve-fireworks-boat-madeira">the New Year's Eve fireworks from the water</a>. For the rest of the season on land, see our guide to <a href="/posts/madeira-in-winter-guide">Madeira in winter</a>.</p>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3>Is December too cold for a boat trip in Madeira?</h3>
<p>No. Average highs in Funchal are about 21°C in December. On the water it feels cooler, so bring a light jacket, especially for the Sunset Trip.</p>
<h3>What time does the Sunset Trip leave in winter?</h3>
<p>It leaves 1 h 15 before that day's sunset: about 16:45 in early December, a little after 17:00 in January and around 17:35 by mid-February.</p>
<h3>How warm is the sea in Madeira in January?</h3>
<p>Roughly 18 to 19°C. It cools to about 18°C in February and March before warming again in spring.</p>
<h3>Are winter trips more likely to be cancelled?</h3>
<p>Yes, more than in summer, mainly because of westerly and south-westerly swell. If the skipper calls it off, you move to a new date or take a full refund.</p>

      <blockquote>In winter the sun stays low, the coast stays quiet, and the cliffs get the best light of the year.</blockquote>
    </article>
